<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Love song · c. 1310 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Chhaap Tilak Sab Chheeni</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="bra">छाप तिलक सब छीनी</bdi> / <bdi lang="bra-Latn">Chhaap Tilak Sab Chheeni</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Sufi song of love for God from the 1300s, said to be by the poet Amir Khusrau, still sung by qawwali singers today.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Amir Khusrau (attributed)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Amir Khusrau (attributed)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Sufis and qawwali singers of the Indian subcontinent</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Braj Bhasha</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>not known (northern India)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>14th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words attributed to Amir Khusrau (14th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhaap-tilak-sab-chheeni">Chhaap Tilak Sab Chheeni</a>&quot; is a 14th-century song in Braj Bhasha. It is credited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mystic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusrau</a> as both poet and composer, and is one of his most popular compositions, heard across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>.</p><p>It is sung above all as qawwali, the devotional music of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a>, by Pakistani and Indian qawwals and other singers. Those who have performed it include Ustad Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan, Naheed Akhtar, Mehnaz Begum, Abida Parveen, the Sabri Brothers, Iqbal Hussain Khan Bandanawazi, Farid Ayaz and Abu Muhammad Qawwal, Jafar Hussain Khan, the sitar and sarod players Ustad Vilayat Khan and Ustad Shujaat Khan, Zila Khan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami</a> Bandhu, Hadiqa Kiani, Smita Bellur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lata-mangeshkar">Lata Mangeshkar</a>, Asha Bhosle, Rahat Fateh Ali Khan, Kailash Kher, Kavita <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a> and Maithili Thakur.</p><p>The song also passed into film and television. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lata-mangeshkar">Lata Mangeshkar</a> and Asha Bhosle sang a version in the 1978 Hindi film Main Tulsi Tere Aangan Ki, and Abida Parveen and Rahat Fateh Ali Khan sang another on the Pakistani music show Coke Studio. It was sung by Keerthi Sagathia, in a setting by Bapi–Tutul, in the film Saat Uchakkey (2016), and by Shishir A Samant and Sunil Kamath in Unpaused (2020).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> song of devotion in which the singer describes being utterly won over by a single glance of the beloved, so that the outward marks of identity and religion fall away. The beloved is read in the Sufi way as the spiritual guide or God. The mood is rapturous and surrendering.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Braj Bhasha</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Braj Bhasha</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bra">छाप तिलक सब छीनी रे मोसे नैना मिलाइके</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bra-Latn">chāp tilak sab chīnī re mose nainā milāike</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You have taken away my every mark and sign, by meeting eyes with me.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bra">बात अगम कह दीनी रे मोसे नैना मिलाइके</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bra-Latn">bāt agam kah dīnī re mose nainā milāike</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You told me a thing beyond reach, by meeting eyes with me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bra">प्रेम भटी का मधवा पिलाइके</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bra-Latn">prem bhaṭī kā madhvā pilāike</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Making me drink the wine from love's still,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bra">मतवाली कर लीनी रे मोसे नैना मिलाइके</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bra-Latn">matvālī kar līnī re mose nainā milāike</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you have made me drunk, by meeting eyes with me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bra">गोरी गोरी बईयाँ, हरी हरी चूड़ियाँ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bra-Latn">gorī gorī baīyāṁ, harī harī cūṛiyāṁ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fair, fair arms, green, green bangles:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bra">बईयाँ पकड़ हर लीनी रे मोसे नैना मिलाइके</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bra-Latn">baīyāṁ pakaṛ har līnī re mose nainā milāike</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you seized my arms and took me, by meeting eyes with me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bra">बल बल जाऊं मैं तोरे रंग रजवा</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bra-Latn">bal bal jāūṁ maiṁ tore raṅg rajvā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I give myself over and over to you, my dyer;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bra">अपनी सी रंग दीनी रे मोसे नैना मिलाइके</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bra-Latn">apnī sī raṅg dīnī re mose nainā milāike</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you have dyed me in your own colour, by meeting eyes with me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bra">ख़ुसरो निजाम के बल बल जाए</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bra-Latn">khusro nijām ke bal bal jāe</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Khusrau gives himself over and over to Nizam;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bra">मोहे सुहागन कीनी रे मोसे नैना मिलाइके</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bra-Latn">mohe suhāgan kīnī re mose nainā milāike</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you have made me a bride, by meeting eyes with me.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="bra">बात अजब कह दीनी रे मोसे नैना मिलाइके</span><span class="sg-r" lang="bra-Latn">bāt ajab kah dīnī re mose nainā milāike</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You told me a wondrous thing, by meeting eyes with me.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Attributed to Amir Khusrau (14th century); the words as sung, as quoted in the Hindi Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://hi.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E0%A4%9B%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%AA_%E0%A4%A4%E0%A4%BF%E0%A4%B2%E0%A4%95_%E0%A4%B8%E0%A4%AC_%E0%A4%9B%E0%A5%80%E0%A4%A8%E0%A5%80" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ISO 15919 transliteration of the Devanagari).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>Line 1.1: chāp and tilak are the caste and sect marks worn on the forehead: the beloved's glance has erased the singer's identity.</li><li>Lines 1.2 and 5.3: agam, beyond reach or understanding, and ajab, wondrous; the &quot;thing&quot; (bāt) is the mystic's secret.</li><li>Line 4.1: the dyer (rajvā, rangrez) runs through Khusrau's poetry: the master dyes the disciple in his own colour.</li><li>Line 5.1: Nizam is Khusrau's Sufi master, Nizamuddin Auliya of Delhi. The song survives in the qawwali tradition, and singers vary its lines and their order.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
